steps.body:
A trie (said "try") stores words letter by letter. Each node has up to 26 children, one per letter, and words that share a prefix share the path from the root. Java has no trie built in, so define the node yourself, after `Main`:

```java
class TrieNode {
    TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26];   // children[0] is 'a', null when missing
    boolean isWord;                           // a word ends at this node
}
System.out.println(new TrieNode().children.length); // 26
```

`c - 'a'` turns a lowercase letter into its slot, 0 to 25. To insert, walk down from the root one letter at a time, create a child wherever one is missing, and mark the last node:

```java harness
class TrieNode { TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26]; boolean isWord; }
TrieNode root = new TrieNode();
{{code}}
insert(root, "cat");
System.out.println(root.children['c' - 'a'].children['a' - 'a'] != null);
```

```java
static void insert(TrieNode root, String word) {
    TrieNode node = root;
    for (char c : word.toCharArray()) {
        int i = c - 'a';
        if (node.children[i] == null) {
            node.children[i] = new TrieNode();
        }
        node = node.children[i];
    }
    node.isWord = true;
}
```

A search walks the same path, and returns `false` as soon as a child is missing. At the end it returns `node.isWord`, not `true`, because reaching the last letter only proves the path exists. After inserting `apple`, the letters of `app` are in the trie, but `app` isn't a word until you insert it too. Both take one step per letter, however many words the trie holds.

