steps.body:
`startsWith(prefix)` is a search without the end check: if the walk gets through the whole prefix, some word continues from there. Both share the walk, so write it once as a helper that returns the node where the prefix ends, or `null`:

```java harness
class TrieNode { TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26]; boolean isWord; int count; }
static void insert(TrieNode root, String w) { TrieNode n = root; for (char c : w.toCharArray()) { if (n.children[c - 'a'] == null) n.children[c - 'a'] = new TrieNode(); n = n.children[c - 'a']; n.count++; } n.isWord = true; }
TrieNode root = new TrieNode();
insert(root, "card");
{{code}}
System.out.println(find(root, "car") != null);
```

```java
static TrieNode find(TrieNode root, String prefix) {
    TrieNode node = root;
    for (char c : prefix.toCharArray()) {
        node = node.children[c - 'a'];
        if (node == null) {
            return null;        // no word goes this way
        }
    }
    return node;
}
```

Then `startsWith` is `find(root, prefix) != null`, and a whole-word search stores `TrieNode node = find(root, word)` and checks `node != null && node.isWord`.

To count the words that start with a prefix, give the node an `int count` and raise it on every node an insert passes through, `node.count++` right after `node = node.children[i]`. A node's `count` is then the number of words at or below it, so the answer is `find(root, prefix).count`, or 0 when `find` returns `null`. Inserting the same word twice counts it twice, so check the search first if words can repeat.

