steps.body:
To list every word under a prefix, the way autocomplete does, walk to the prefix's node with `find`, then run a DFS below it. Carry the letters so far in a `StringBuilder`: append a letter before going down to a child, and delete it on the way back. That is choose, explore, unchoose from the Depth-First Search lesson.

```java harness
class TrieNode { TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26]; boolean isWord; int count; }
static void insert(TrieNode root, String w) { TrieNode n = root; for (char c : w.toCharArray()) { if (n.children[c - 'a'] == null) n.children[c - 'a'] = new TrieNode(); n = n.children[c - 'a']; n.count++; } n.isWord = true; }
TrieNode root = new TrieNode();
for (String w : new String[]{"tea", "ten", "to"}) insert(root, w);
List<String> out = new ArrayList<>();
{{code}}
collect(root, new StringBuilder(), out);
System.out.println(out);
```

```java
static void collect(TrieNode node, StringBuilder path, List<String> out) {
    if (node.isWord) {
        out.add(path.toString());
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
        if (node.children[i] != null) {
            path.append((char) ('a' + i));          // choose
            collect(node.children[i], path, out);   // explore
            path.deleteCharAt(path.length() - 1);   // unchoose
        }
    }
}
```

`(char) ('a' + i)` turns slot `i` back into its letter. Without the cast, `'a' + i` is an `int` and the builder appends a number.

The loop visits the children from `a` to `z`, so the words come out in alphabetical order with no sort. A word that is a prefix of another, like `tea` before `team`, comes first, because a node records its own word before it visits its children. Start the path with the prefix itself, `new StringBuilder(prefix)`, so every collected word is complete.

