steps.body:
A pattern where `.` stands for any one letter can't follow a single path. At a `.`, try every child and succeed if any of them matches the rest of the pattern. That's a recursive DFS that carries an index into the pattern:

```java harness
class TrieNode { TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[26]; boolean isWord; int count; }
static void insert(TrieNode root, String w) { TrieNode n = root; for (char c : w.toCharArray()) { if (n.children[c - 'a'] == null) n.children[c - 'a'] = new TrieNode(); n = n.children[c - 'a']; n.count++; } n.isWord = true; }
TrieNode root = new TrieNode();
for (String w : new String[]{"bad", "dad"}) insert(root, w);
{{code}}
System.out.println(matches(root, ".ad", 0) + " " + matches(root, "b.", 0));
```

```java
static boolean matches(TrieNode node, String pattern, int i) {
    if (node == null) {
        return false;                   // no word goes this way
    }
    if (i == pattern.length()) {
        return node.isWord;             // the whole pattern is used up
    }
    char c = pattern.charAt(i);
    if (c != '.') {
        return matches(node.children[c - 'a'], pattern, i + 1);
    }
    for (TrieNode child : node.children) {
        if (matches(child, pattern, i + 1)) {
            return true;                // one match is enough
        }
    }
    return false;
}
```

The `null` check at the top handles missing children, so the loop can pass every slot, `null` ones included, without a test of its own. A plain letter follows one child as before, so a pattern with no dots costs one step per letter. Each dot can multiply the work by up to 26, which is still small for short patterns. The recursion is only as deep as the pattern is long.

